  "methodology": "Compute search-to-Caddy-total fractions for each observed time window. Place each fraction at the window midpoint and linearly interpolate in local time, wrapping from the night observation to the next morning. Weight each day's interpolated fractions by its observed 15-minute total-traffic profile. Multiply that traffic-weighted fraction by the day's 24-hour Caddy counter increase. Average the modeled daily search volumes over 30 complete days.",
  "assumptions": [
    "The four search-to-total fractions observed on October 27 are representative of those times on other days.",
    "Search share between sampled times, including unobserved overnight hours, changes smoothly according to periodic linear interpolation.",
    "The Caddy metric measures a sufficiently comparable all-endpoint traffic population throughout the period. The same Caddy denominator is used in both observed ratios and historical traffic weighting."
  ],
  "limitations": [
